{
 "id": "french-defence",
 "name": "French Defence",
 "kind": "opening",
 "track": "openings",
 "level": "intermediate",
 "goal": "As Black against 1.e4, you can play the French Defence and know the plan against White's pawn chain: attack its base with c5 and its front with f6.",
 "priorKnowledge": [
  "pawn-structure"
 ],
 "side": "black",
 "eco": "C02",
 "puzzleTags": [
  "French_Defense"
 ],
 "puzzleThemes": [],
 "intro": {
  "text": "In the French Defence, 1...e6 and 2...d5, you let White build a big centre and then attack it. In the Advance Variation, White pushes a pawn to e5, making a chain of pawns on d4 and e5 that takes space on the kingside. Your plan is the same in almost every line: hit the back of the chain, d4, with ...c5 and your pieces, then hit its front, e5, with ...f6, until White's centre gives way and your pieces get room. The price is the bishop on c8, which stays shut in behind the e6 pawn for a while."
 },
